+From e99498e917c1d266c2f806c00992a3ff282808a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
+Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 04:04:04 -0700
+Subject: [PATCH] sia_storage download: keep recovered shards at their slab
+ index over the slab's full shard count
+
+`SlabRecovery::new` numbers each sector by its position in the slab, then `prioritize` drops every sector whose host
+the SDK does not know. `Hosts::refresh` replaces the host list with the indexer's USABLE hosts, so this happens
+whenever an indexer marks or blocks a host unusable.
+
+`recover_shards` then sized the shard vector by the REMAINING sectors and indexed it by the ORIGINAL index:
+- one unknown host at a lower index made index 29 out of bounds: download.rs:336 panics, and the wasm component traps;
+- `decode` derived the parity count from that same shortened length.
+
+Fix:
+- `AwaitingRecovery` carries the slab's shard count (`total_shards`, read before `prioritize`).
+- The vector covers every shard, so `decode` keeps the slab's 10+20 shape.
+- A recovered shard is stored only at an in-range index that is not already filled; otherwise the recovery fails with
+  `InvalidSlab`, instead of panicking or overwriting.
+- Nothing else changes: sector proofs, decryption, the erasure code, retries and NotEnoughShards.
+
+New `download::test::shard_topology` tests, through `SlabRecovery` and the public `Download` reader, on a real 10+20
+slab over the mock network:
+- parity-only non-contiguous recovery (hosts of 0-2 unknown, 3-19 unreadable);
+- trailing hosts unknown;
+- one unknown host with out-of-order completion;
+- single-host loss at 8 positions;
+- insufficient shards: a clean NotEnoughShards;
+- malformed (out-of-range or duplicate) indices: InvalidSlab.
+
+On the baseline source (negctl/) five of the six panic at download.rs:336:35.
